# What happens when the government sets a minimum price?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Why do price floors create surpluses, from butter mountains to unsold labour? Who gains and who loses?"
next: {"title":"How does a price coordinate millions who never meet?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/20-how-does-a-price-coordinate-millions-who.cast"}

## The butter mountain
sources:
    - id: hansard1988
      title: Milk Quotas and Butter Mountains, HL Deb 12 December 1988 vol 502 cc757-8
      authors: UK House of Lords (Baroness Trumpington, MAFF)
      year: 1988
      finding: EC intervention stocks of butter fell from 1,323,000 tonnes in August 1986 to about 146,000 tonnes in December 1988, thanks to milk quotas (from 1984) and stock disposal.
      url: https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/lords/1988/dec/12/milk-quotas-and-butter-mountains
    - id: cvce1986
      title: '''As of yesterday the EEC has a population of 321 million'', Luxemburger Wort, 1 January 1986'
      authors: CVCE
      year: 1986
      finding: With Spain and Portugal, the twelve-member Community had about 321 million inhabitants (1.32 million tonnes ≈ 4 kg each).
      url: https://www.cvce.eu/en/obj/as_of_yesterday_the_eec_has_a_population_of_321_million_from_the_luxemburger_wort_1_january_1986-en-0fce8985-e6ce-4d38-9855-7aa96bfcc4cb.html

    icon butter size 150 set twemoji x 300 y 610 or ["cheese","milk"] icon_look picture of butter hidden true
    icon cold size 70 set twemoji x 420 y 590 or ["ice"] icon_look picture of snowflake hidden true
    text cue_floor "guaranteed\nminimum price" x 650 y 610 font_size 40 hidden true
    scale stores cites ["hansard1988"] y 320 font_size 30 label "EC butter stocks, Aug 1986" unit "m tonnes" min 0 max 2 value 1.32 ticks 5 markers [{"value":0.15,"label":"Dec 1988: 0.15"}] hidden true
    text cue_kg "≈ 4 kg per person" cites ["cvce1986"] x 790 y 480 font_size 36 hidden true
    text cue_quota "1984: milk quotas" cites ["hansard1988"] x 790 y 480 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "The butter mountain"

What happens when a government promises that a price will never fall below a certain level? Europe tried it with butter.
    draw butter

B: From the 1960s, the European Com (@draw cue_floor@)munity guaranteed dairy farmers a minimum price. Butter that shoppers would not buy at that pri (@draw cold@)ce, the Community bought itself.

B: And put it in cold stores. So here's the question: at the peak, in 1986, how much butter was sitting in those stores?
    draw stores

    ask "Click where you think the stock stood in August 1986." on stores store g default "0.3" tolerance 0.2
        intro: Click your guess on the line.
        right: Close: you said {g}; it was {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; it was {g.true}.

About one point three million tonnes, in August 1986.
    highlight stores

B: That's around four kilos for every person in the Community. The papers called it the butter mountain.
    draw cue_kg

    pause 0.3

Nobody planned a mountain. It grew out of one rule: a price that is not allowed to fall.
    highlight cue_floor effect underline

B: So how did they get rid of it? Cut the price?
    erase cue_kg

Not mainly. From 1984 t (@draw cue_quota@)hey capped how much milk each farmer could sell. By December 1988 the stores were down (@draw stores_marker_1@) to about a hundred and fifty thousand tonnes.

    pause 0.5

Why would capping production melt a mountain made by a price? To see it, we need to draw the floor.
    highlight cue_floor effect box

    pause 0.3

## Who gains, who loses
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Fish, kg a week (Q)
    y_label: Price, $ a kg (P)
    demand:
      curvature: linear
      elasticity: 0.896
    supply:
      curvature: linear
      elasticity: 0.958
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
      p_label: $5
      q_label: '500'
    price_floor:
      level: 70
      label: Minimum price
      show_surplus: true
    regions:
      - transfer
      - deadweight_loss
    units:
      price: [0, 10]
      quantity: [0, 1000]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: kg
    readout:
      - qd
      - qs
    readout_at: inside

    curve zero expr "0" hidden true
    region taxpayer #c96567 x_from 30 x_to 70 between ["floor_line","zero"] style.fill #c96567 hidden true
    text label_tax "taxpayers" at.data [61,22] font_size 28 hidden true
    text tick7 "$7" at.data [-5,70] font_size 22 hidden true

    card title "Who gains, who loses"

A floor like that is easiest to see in a market we can draw. Say Harbourtown's fish, with illustrative prices.
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S

B: Left alone, buyers and fishers settle at five dollars a kilo and five hundred kilos a week.
    draw equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar

Then the harbour council promises fishers at least seven dollars a kilo.
    draw floor_line label_floor tick7

B: At seven, buyers want less: say three hundred kilos.
    draw surplus_guide_qd label_Qd readout_qd

And fishers, paid more, bring in more: seven hundred.
    draw surplus_guide_qs label_Qs readout_qs

B: So four hundred kilos a week that nobody buys at that price. That's the surplus: Europe's butter, in fish.
    draw surplus_arrow label_surplus

    pause 0.3

Who gains? The fishers who still sell get two dollars more on every kilo, taken straight from buyers.
    draw transfer_region label_transfer

B: And buyers lose more than that: the fish they no longer buy at all.  (@draw dwl_region@)Trades that would have helped both sides simply vanish.

That lost part go (@highlight label_DWL@)es to nobody. It's the deadweight loss.
    draw label_DWL

B: And if the council buys up the surp (@draw taxpayer label_tax@)lus to keep its promise, taxpayers pay seven dollars a kilo for fish nobody wanted.

So fishers gain, while buyers and ta (@highlight label_DWL label_tax@)xpayers lose more than fishers gain.
    highlight label_transfer

    erase taxpayer label_tax

    erase readout tick7

Pause and drag the floor yourself. Push it below five dollars, then raise it slowly.
    explore

B: Below the market price it does nothing at all. Abov (@animate price_floor.level 80 duration 2.5@)e it, every dollar makes the pile bigger.
    animate price_floor.level 40 duration 2

    pause 0.4

## A floor under wages
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Workers (L)
    y_label: Wage (w)
    demand:
      curvature: linear
      elasticity: 0.896
      label: D (firms)
    supply:
      curvature: linear
      elasticity: 0.958
      label: S (workers)
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: false
      p_label: w*
      q_label: L*
    price_floor:
      level: 70
      label: Minimum wage
      show_surplus: true
    box:
      x: 10
      'y': 150
      w: 640
      h: 510
sources:
    - id: ck1994
      title: 'Minimum Wages and Employment: A Case Study of the Fast-Food Industry in New Jersey and Pennsylvania'
      authors: David Card and Alan B. Krueger
      year: 1994
      finding: After New Jersey raised its minimum wage from $4.25 to $5.05 on 1 April 1992, a survey of about 400 fast-food restaurants in New Jersey and eastern Pennsylvania found no fall in New Jersey employment relative to Pennsylvania.
      doi: 10.3386/w4509
    - id: nw2000
      title: 'Minimum Wages and Employment: A Case Study of the Fast-Food Industry in New Jersey and Pennsylvania: Comment'
      authors: David Neumark and William Wascher
      year: 2000
      finding: Re-estimated with restaurants' payroll records, the New Jersey increase was associated with lower employment.
    - id: econlib
      title: Airline Deregulation
      authors: Fred L. Smith Jr. and Braden Cox, The Concise Encyclopedia of Economics (Econlib)
      year: 2008
      finding: 'Under Civil Aeronautics Board fare regulation, fares and flight frequency were high and load factors low: in the early 1970s only about 50 percent; airlines competed on service.'
      url: https://www.econlib.org/library/Enc/AirlineDeregulation.html

    text cue_unemp "unemployed" at.data [50,90] font_size 26 hidden true
    scratch nj cites ["ck1994"] work ["New Jersey, 1992","$4.25 → $5.05","fast-food jobs: no fall"] x 815 y 330 font_size 24 hidden true
    portrait card x 850 y 560 width 200 of "David Card" cameo true hidden true
    scratch mono work ["one big employer","sets w too low","floor: more pay, more jobs"] x 815 y 330 font_size 24 hidden true
    scratch sure cites ["nw2000"] work ["goods floors → surplus:","consensus","minimum wage → jobs:","contested"] x 815 y 330 font_size 24 hidden true
    scratch math work ["D: Q = 1000 − 100P","S: Q = 100P","→ P = $5, Q = 500","P = $7: Qd = 300, Qs = 700","400 kg × $7 = $2,800 a week"] x 500 y 420 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "A floor under wages"

    hide label_surplus

B: Here's where it gets political. Put the same floor under the price of labour, and what do you get?
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E

A mini (@draw floor_line label_floor@)mum wage. The simple picture says firms hir (@draw surplus_guide_qd label_Qd surplus_guide_qs label_Qs@)e fewer workers than want jobs at that wage, and the ga (@draw surplus_arrow cue_unemp@)p is unemployment.

    hide label_surplus label_surplus_leader

B: But is that what happens? In 1992 New Jersey (@draw nj_box nj_line_1@) raised its minimum wage from four twenty-fi (@draw nj_line_2@)ve to five oh five an hour. Pennsylvania next door didn't.

David Card and Alan Krueger surveyed fast-food restaurants on both sides of the border. They found no f (@draw nj_line_3@)all in jobs in New Jersey.
    draw card

B: So the picture is wrong?
    highlight surplus_arrow cue_unemp

    erase nj card

Maybe the market in it is. If one big employer, like (@draw mono_box mono_line_1 mono_line_2@) Harbourtown's boatyard, sets wages itself, it pays below the competitive wag (@fade surplus_arrow cue_unemp surplus_guide_qd surplus_guide_qs label_Qd label_Qs to 0.2@)e, and a floor ca (@draw mono_line_3@)n raise both pay and jobs.

B: Others re-ran the study with payroll rec (@fade surplus_arrow cue_unemp surplus_guide_qd surplus_guide_qs label_Qd label_Qs to 1@)ords and found job losses.
    erase mono

B: So how sure are we? That flo (@draw sure_box sure_line_1 sure_line_2@)ors on goods pile up surpluses: consensus.

B: What a moderate minimum wage does to jobs: contested.
    draw sure_line_3 sure_line_4

    pause 0.4

    erase sure

    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test

    ask "Show the surplus worked out, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want to see the fish surplus worked out?

@math

    fade axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E floor_line label_floor surplus_guide_qd label_Qd surplus_guide_qs label_Qs surplus_arrow cue_unemp to 0.08

    hide mathq

Say demand is Q equals a thousand minus a hundred P, and supply is Q equals a hundred P. They meet at five dollars and five hundred kilos.
    draw math_box math_line_1 math_line_2 math_line_3

At seven dollars, buyers take three hundred kilos and fishers bring seven hundred.
    draw math_line_4

Four hundred kilos left over; bought up at seven dollars, that's two thousand eight hundred dollars of public money every week.
    draw math_line_5

    erase math

@test

    fade axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E floor_line label_floor surplus_guide_qd label_Qd surplus_guide_qs label_Qs surplus_arrow cue_unemp to 1

    hide mathq

    quiz "A country sets a minimum price for wheat below what wheat sells for anyway. What happens?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: Nothing: a floor below the market price binds nobody.
        wrong: Does a floor bind anyone if the market price is already above it?
        + Nothing changes
        * A wheat surplus
        * A wheat shortage

    quiz "Before 1978, US regulators kept air fares high and airlines could not undercut them. What would you expect to see?" voice b
        right: Empty seats: the surplus. In the early 1970s planes flew about half full, and airlines competed with meals and service instead of price.
        wrong: At a price held above the market, who wants less?
        + Many empty seats
        * Planes always full
        * Fares falling anyway

    quiz "A minimum price for milk, and nobody buys up what is left over. Which farmers gain?"
        right: Those who still sell: the higher price is paid only on milk somebody buys; the rest is left unsold.
        wrong: The higher price is only paid on milk somebody buys.
        + Those who still sell
        * Every dairy farmer
        * None of them

So a minimum price never just raises a price. It decides who still sells, who stops buying, and who pays for the pile.
    highlight floor_line surplus_arrow

And whether a floor under wages works like one under butter depends on how competitive the market was to begin with.
    highlight demand_curve

    pause 0.8

